        <!-- 9. LEADER TRAINING -->
        <div v-show="currentView === 'training' && !loading" class="space-y-8 pb-10">
            <div class="glass bg-cardDark p-8 rounded-3xl border-t-4 border-t-accent shadow-[0_10px_40px_rgba(0,0,0,0.2)]">
                <div class="mb-8 border-b border-white/10 pb-6">
                    <span class="text-accent font-bold tracking-wider uppercase text-xs mb-2 block">Leader Preparation Guide</span>
                    <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-white mb-2">Planting a Disciple Making Church</h2>
                    <p class="text-slate-300">By Bobby Harrington, Point Leader, Discipleship.org</p>
                </div>

                <div class="space-y-8 text-slate-300 leading-relaxed text-sm md:text-base">
                    <section class="space-y-4">
                        <p><strong>Dear Discipleship-First Friends,</strong></p>
                        <p>One of the most important questions church planters must answer is this: <strong>What kind of church are we trying to create?</strong></p>
                        <p>If we want to plant churches that make disciples who make disciples, then we must begin by making disciples who make disciples. The methods we use at the beginning will establish the DNA of the church for years to come.</p>
                        <p>Too often, church planting begins with a focus on launching a Sunday gathering. Leaders spend months raising money, building teams, and preparing for a public launch. Sunday gatherings matter. Corporate praise matters. But there is a danger.</p>
                        <div class="bg-red-500/10 border-l-4 border-l-red-500 p-4 rounded-r-xl my-6">
                            <p class="text-red-300 font-semibold mb-1">The Danger</p>
                            <p class="text-red-200/80 text-sm">When the primary focus is launching a Sunday service, disciple making often becomes something we hope to add later. It is very difficult to turn that into a culture. The result is that many churches become excellent at gathering crowds but struggle to develop disciples.</p>
                        </div>
                        <p>What if we reversed the process? What if church planting started with disciple making before it started with Sunday gatherings?</p>
                    </section>

                    <section class="space-y-4 pt-6 border-t border-white/10">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white text-accent">Be What You Want the Church to Become</h3>
                        <p>Every church planter (and minister/pastor) reproduces who they are. The church plant will be more effected by the lead planter than by any strategy, program, or ministry model. People will imitate what they see.</p>
                        <p>Before we gather a crowd, we must personally invest in and disciple people in smaller communities. Before we build systems, we must model obedience. Church planters should never outsource disciple making to a future ministry leader.</p>
                        <p class="font-bold text-white bg-white/5 p-4 rounded-lg inline-block">The church will become what its leaders consistently practice.</p>
                    </section>

                    <section class="space-y-4 pt-6 border-t border-white/10">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white text-accent">Start with Simple and Reproducible Disciple Making Communities</h3>
                        <p>The first step is not launching a service. It is making disciples. We begin by gathering a simple disciple making group. In these disciple-making communities, people learn to:</p>
                        <ul class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-4">
                            <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> Care for and support each other</li>
                            <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> Follow Jesus together</li>
                            <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> Obey His teachings</li>
                            <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> Pray (and fast) together</li>
                            <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> Share their faith</li>
                            <li class="flex items-center gap-2"><svg class="w-5 h-5 text-accent" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M5 13l4 4L19 7"></path></svg> Reproduce groups</li>
                        </ul>
                        <p class="mt-4">Because simplicity drives multiplication, these groups should be easy to reproduce. The goal is reaching lost people and spiritual reproduction.</p>
                    </section>

                    <section class="space-y-4 pt-6 border-t border-white/10">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white text-accent">Reproduce Before You Gather</h3>
                        <p>A critical principle in planting a disciple making church is this: <strong>Reproduce Multiple Disciple Making Communities Before Launching Larger Tuesday Gatherings.</strong></p>
                        <div class="bg-black/30 p-6 rounded-xl border border-white/10 space-y-4 my-6">
                            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                                <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-accent text-black font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0">1</div>
                                <p>A leader starts two disciple making communities.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                                <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-accent text-black font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0">2</div>
                                <p>New disciples are developed and equipped.</p>
                            </div>
                            <div class="flex items-start gap-4">
                                <div class="w-8 h-8 rounded-full bg-accent text-black font-bold flex items-center justify-center shrink-0">3</div>
                                <p>Those disciples launch new communities. New leaders emerge.</p>
                            </div>
                        </div>
                        <p>By the time a larger church gathering begins, the expectation that everyday disciples make disciples is already deeply rooted in the culture.</p>
                    </section>

                    <section class="space-y-4 pt-6 border-t border-white/10">
                        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white text-accent">The End Goal</h3>
                        <p>The goal is not merely to plant a church. The goal is to plant a disciple making church. We want disciples who make disciples, who make more disciples, who then gather together in larger Sunday gatherings. Then they make more disciple making disciples who plant more churches… and on and on it goes.</p>
                        <p>When disciple making comes first, Sunday gatherings become stronger. Leadership becomes healthier. Mission becomes clearer. Multiplication becomes normal.</p>
                    </section>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
